  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1250 PM Tuesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A coastal low lifting northward along the Southeast \nUS and Carolina coasts is expected to bring rain to central NC this \nweekend.   \n\nAloft, as the mid-level anticyclone shifts east and offshore on Fri, \na srn stream upper level s/w will move ewd across the Gulf Coast. As \nthe s/w lifts across the Southeast US Fri night/Sat, a closed low \nwill develop in the lower levels, along/off the east coast of s-cent \nFL, deepening as it lifts nwd along the Southeast US, Carolina, and \npossibly mid-Atlantic coasts Sat and Sun. At the surface, a low is \nexpected to develop near/north of Cuba Wed night/Thu, then deepen as \nit lifts nwd between FL and the Caribbean on Fri. The low should \ncontinue to deepen as it lifts nwd off the Southeast US and Carolina \ncoasts Fri night and Sat, with a secondary low potentially \ndeveloping to the east of the primary low on Sat. Uncertainty \nincreases thereafter wrt track and timing of the primary low, but \nfor now much of central NC appears to remain on the cool/northern \nside of the low, less confidence in that with east/sewd extent. The \noperational GFS is more progressive and tracks more northeastward \nand slightly inland from the EC, while the latter is subsequently \nquicker with the cold front passage in the wake of the low early \nnext week. \n\nPrecipitation: Increasing confidence in onset of rain from the south-\nsoutheast Sat aft/eve, with the highest chances for rain, possibly \nheavy at times, area-wide on Sun. The GFS is quicker with rain \nexiting the area Mon night, but has more run-to-run continuity than \nthe slower EC solution. Based on the current track of the low, do \nnot expect much convective activity across most of cntl NC, though \nthat potential increases with sewd extent, and given the continued \nuncertainty, cannot be ruled out at this time.",
